1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,260
Venus is the planet most like Earth. So

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:04,340
what happened to it?

3
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:07,400
This is the ground on Venus seen through

4
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:11,040
the clouds by radar. Why radar? Because

5
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:13,620
ordinary light cannot get through. It is

6
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:16,740
hot enough to melt lead. The air presses

7
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:19,000
down like the sea nearly a kilometer deep.

8
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:21,340
No machine we have landed there could

9
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:23,520
survive it for much more than two hours.

10
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:26,520
And this world is almost the size of

11
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:29,580
yours. It is made of the same rock. It

12
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:31,560
even takes in less sunlight than Earth

13
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:34,220
does, and it is still the hottest planet

14
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:35,240
in the solar system.

15
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,060
In this film, I will show you how that can

16
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:41,260
be true, where its water went, and why the

17
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:42,840
answer is still open.

18
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:45,980
Start with what the two planets share.

19
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:49,960
Venus is ninety-five percent as wide as

20
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:52,960
Earth, with about four-fifths of its mass.

21
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:56,300
It formed from the same kind of rock in

22
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:59,340
the same young solar system at nearly the

23
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:00,120
same time.

24
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:03,540
From a distance, it is the closest thing

25
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:05,700
to a second Earth that we know.

26
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:09,040
It is also closer to the sun.

27
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:12,560
Venus receives almost twice the sunlight

28
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:13,540
that reaches us.

29
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:17,020
So the easy answer is that it is simply

30
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:18,840
nearer the fire.

31
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:22,840
But that answer breaks on one number.

32
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:27,040
Venus is wrapped in bright cloud that

33
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:29,400
reflects about three-quarters of that

34
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:31,600
sunlight straight back to space.

35
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:35,320
So Venus actually soaks up less sunlight

36
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:39,180
than Earth does. Less light in and still a

37
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,400
surface at four hundred and sixty-five

38
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:42,100
degrees.

39
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:46,240
It sounds like a paradox. It stops being

40
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:48,220
one when you look at the air.

41
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:52,060
So the heat is not about how much light

42
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:54,680
arrives. It is about what happens to the

43
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:58,640
heat once it is there. It cannot get out.

44
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:01,580
What would you see if you stood there?

45
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:06,200
Not darkness. The clouds let through a dim

46
00:02:06,260 --> 00:02:08,919
orange daylight like a heavy evening that

47
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:09,780
never ends.

48
00:02:10,699 --> 00:02:12,620
You would never see the sun itself.

49
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,580
The wind at the ground moves at little

50
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:18,660
more than a slow walk. But in air that

51
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:21,860
thick, even a slow wind pushes like a

52
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:24,980
river. The air of Venus is ninety-six

53
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,300
percent carbon dioxide, and there is a

54
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:30,220
great deal of it. Carbon dioxide lets

55
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:32,520
sunlight through but blocks the heat

56
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:36,040
trying to leave. On Earth, it is a thin

57
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:38,580
trace in the air, and even that trace

58
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:42,300
keeps the planet warm. On Venus, the whole

59
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:45,500
sky is made of it, ninety times thicker

60
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:49,080
than ours. The heat goes in. It does not

61
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:49,740
come out.

62
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:53,900
Day and night, pole and equator, the

63
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:55,720
ground sits at nearly the same

64
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:56,360
temperature.

65
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:58,460
Here is the turn.

66
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:01,580
Earth has roughly as much carbon dioxide

67
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:04,840
as Venus. It is just not in the air. It is

68
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:08,300
in rock, limestone, chalk, and seabed

69
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:12,000
built over billions of years. Rain and

70
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,940
rivers carry it to the sea, and sea life

71
00:03:14,980 --> 00:03:17,100
and chemistry lock it into the floor.

72
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:20,100
The thing that pulled Earth's carbon out

73
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:23,040
of the sky was water. So the question

74
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:26,320
changes. It is not why Venus has so much

75
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:29,620
carbon dioxide. It is why Venus has no

76
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:31,620
water to put it away.

77
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:34,280
We make one of these every week.

78
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:37,500
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

79
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:41,460
The water is the mystery, and Venus left a

80
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:43,740
clue in the air about where it went.

81
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:47,720
In 1978, a NASA probe fell through the

82
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:50,420
clouds of Venus and tasted the air on the

83
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:53,960
way down. It found a trace of water and

84
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:55,680
something odd inside it.

85
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:59,220
Some hydrogen is heavier than the rest. It

86
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:00,660
is called deuterium.

87
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,360
On Venus, the share of heavy hydrogen is

88
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:06,240
around a hundred times higher than on

89
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:09,100
Earth. That number is a fingerprint.

90
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:12,520
High in the air, sunlight breaks water

91
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:15,740
apart. The light hydrogen escapes to space

92
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:17,660
more easily than the heavy kind.

93
00:04:18,579 --> 00:04:21,140
Lose enough water that way over a long

94
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:23,740
enough time, and the heavy hydrogen is

95
00:04:23,780 --> 00:04:24,700
what remains.

96
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:29,000
Venus has lost a great deal of water. How

97
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:32,000
much and in what form is still unknown.

98
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:34,120
Was it ever an ocean?

99
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:39,060
Spin matters here. Venus turns once every

100
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:41,380
two hundred and forty-three Earth days,

101
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:43,280
and it turns backwards.

102
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:46,280
A single day there is longer than its

103
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:47,060
whole year.

104
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:50,420
That slow turn decides where the clouds

105
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:50,780
gather.

106
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:55,600
One model says it may have been. In 2016,

107
00:04:55,940 --> 00:04:58,900
a team at NASA's Goddard Institute ran

108
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,800
early Venus with that slow spin and found

109
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:04,300
that thick daytime clouds could shade the

110
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:07,900
ground. In their model, Venus may have had

111
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:10,600
shallow oceans and mild weather for

112
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:11,900
billions of years.

113
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:15,160
Another model says the water never came

114
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:20,120
down. In 2021, a team led by Martin Turbet

115
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:22,360
found that clouds gathered on the night

116
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:25,360
side instead, trapping heat like a lid.

117
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:28,540
The steam stayed in the sky, and the

118
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:30,480
oceans may never have formed.

119
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:33,440
Both are careful models, and they

120
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:34,140
disagree.

121
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:37,280
Nobody has yet read the rocks that could

122
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:37,940
settle it.

123
00:05:38,980 --> 00:05:42,040
Either way, Venus ended in the same place.

124
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:46,540
Water gone, carbon in the sky, heat with

125
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:50,120
nowhere to go. So why Venus and not Earth?

126
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:53,500
Distance is part of it. Nearer the sun,

127
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:55,360
there is more light to start the climb.

128
00:05:55,940 --> 00:05:58,540
Warmer air holds more water vapor, and

129
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,380
water vapor is itself a greenhouse gas.

130
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:04,420
Past a certain line, the steam reaches

131
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:06,940
high enough for sunlight to split it, and

132
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:09,460
the water starts leaking away to space.

133
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:12,900
Venus sits on the wrong side of that line.

134
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,340
Earth sits on the safe side for now.

135
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:19,340
Last week, we saw the sun getting

136
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:21,900
brighter, about one percent every hundred

137
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:25,100
and ten million years. That climb moves

138
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:27,600
the line outward. In about a billion

139
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:30,360
years, it reaches Earth. This is not a

140
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:32,980
warning about next century. The warming

141
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:35,440
measured on Earth today is real, and it is

142
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:36,480
a different ledger.

143
00:06:37,420 --> 00:06:39,420
Studies of Earth's climate find that

144
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:42,120
burning our fossil fuels is very unlikely

145
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:44,260
to start a full runaway like this.

146
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:47,680
Venus is a story told in hundreds of

147
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:50,840
millions of years. What it shows is how

148
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:54,120
thin the difference is. Same rock, same

149
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:57,760
start, and one turn with the water. The

150
00:06:57,820 --> 00:06:59,680
answer is waiting on the ground.

151
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:03,020
Venus has repainted most of its surface

152
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:05,140
with lava in the last billion years or so.

153
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:07,860
But some high crumpled regions called

154
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:09,280
tesserae look older.

155
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:12,820
If water ever shaped Venus, rocks like

156
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:14,380
those may still remember it.

157
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:16,560
People have landed there.

158
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:20,660
In 1982, a Soviet lander photographed the

159
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:23,100
ground and lasted about two hours before

160
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:23,960
the heat won.

161
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:28,280
In 2021, a probe on its way to the sun

162
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:30,820
looked at the night side of Venus and saw

163
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:32,980
the faint glow of the hot surface through

164
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:33,700
the clouds.

165
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:36,540
The next step is to read the rocks

166
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:37,100
properly.

167
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:40,420
NASA chose two missions to go back.

168
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:43,500
DAVINCI, designed to drop through the air

169
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:45,640
and photograph the old highlands of Alpha

170
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:49,040
Regio, and VERITAS, designed to map the

171
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:51,260
surface and its rock types from orbit.

172
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:54,580
Europe has its own orbiter, EnVision.

173
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,780
The planet next door is the clearest

174
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:00,560
record we have of how a world like ours

175
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:04,400
can turn, not by falling apart, by keeping

176
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:06,800
its heat and losing its water.

177
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:11,020
What if Venus was never the monster, but a

178
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:13,960
twin that simply ran the experiment first?

179
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:17,460
The bigger question hangs in your own sky,

180
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:20,220
the brightest thing at dawn or dusk after

181
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:20,780
the moon.

182
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:24,480
How close did Earth come to the same turn?

183
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:28,400
Next week, what happens to your clock near

184
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:29,500
the speed of light?
